      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/17a493e38294911702a94b8755111db85e7445f03d4d149602b57eebf009f14b.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have that \n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/e995ce9652d431c39c7a629a5e825c91fcdb2da56b83ad371c6bb319bc548eb9.png)\n\nThen, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}$ and ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/fa491500bff1c1e9d0f8453552f33b8deed066e3799a36817d039fc625eee47d.png)\n\nAs a result, ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}$ is the following: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/fbcbb5ebd87fef90163ce5a358beb7bc40b4c4b3d91b89684500577ccedf2b13.png)\n\nFinally, to get the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$ or ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/01776646145493da01e17a764ee1b71161595bcc249f376fc18cd9325a8218e7.png)",
